{
  "name": "app-design",
  "pass": [
    "fits"
  ],
  "question": "The input has three sections. [CARD] is the requirement card a human holds: what the human wants and the answers the human has settled on. [SCREENS] is what the finished app declares: its name and description, and each screen with its key, label and the sizes it is drawn at. [SCREEN_SOURCE] is the source code of the app's screens (the front-end files under src/screens, possibly cut off). Decide whether the screen design fits the app's purpose for this person. Look at what the person would actually see and do on the screens, not at how the code is written. For a calendar, a screen that fits lets the person see their schedule laid out by date (for example a month, week or day view, or a day-by-day agenda with dates) and work from it, and carries what the card says the person needs (adding, viewing and removing, reminders, and any model or behaviour the card settles, such as recurring definitions drawn virtually or an occurrence turned into a stored item when it gets an action). A screen that is only a generic list or table of records with an add form and delete buttons, with no date or calendar layout, does not fit. Verdicts: fits = the screens give the person a purpose-fitting view and the main things on the card can be done from them; generic = the screens are a generic list or form that would look the same for any kind of app, or a main thing on the card cannot be done from the screens; cannot_tell = the input does not let you decide (for example no screens or no source). The quote must be the part of the input that decided your verdict (for fits, the declared screen or the source line that shows the date or calendar layout; for generic, the screen or source line that shows the plain list or the missing layout).",
  "verdicts": [
    "fits",
    "generic",
    "cannot_tell"
  ]
}
